# Exhaustive Walkthrough: Enterprise Settings Module

The Enterprise Settings Module has been completely implemented from end-to-end. This is a deeply detailed account of the architecture, the hierarchical engine, the specific data models, and the API endpoints that power the configuration of the KapuLetu ecosystem.

> [!NOTE]
> This module was explicitly built to prevent "clashing." Instead of forcing the frontend to juggle massive JSON blobs, the entire system is built around strict domain isolation and explicit button-mapped endpoints.

---

## 1. Zero-Clash Database Architecture

To support the massive volume of settings without creating an unmanageable 40+ column SQL table, the database schema was augmented with strictly validated `JSON` columns.

- **`models/users.py`**: Added the `preferences` (JSON) column to hold the Global User settings.
- **`models/group.py`**: Added the `settings_override` (JSON) column to hold local Group overrides.
- **`models/campaign.py`**: Added the `settings_override` (JSON) column to hold local Campaign overrides.

### Domain Isolation Execution
When a user updates their security timeout via `/settings/me/security/timeout`, the backend uses SQLAlchemy's `flag_modified` to extract, mutate, and re-save **only** the `security` dictionary inside the JSON. This mathematically guarantees that updating security will never accidentally overwrite or clash with automation or billing settings.

